digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate an (NH4)BiI3-based optical floating-gate transistor non-volatile optoelectronic memory that replaces the metal control gate with a dielectric 2D photoconductive layer to enable gate-voltage-free light-driven multi-level non-volatile synaptic states for low-energy neuromorphic training and inference.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A viable optoelectronic neuromorphic memory proposal should connect system needs, an architecture that enables gate-voltage-free optical programming, a material satisfying photosensitive-control-gate criteria, and device/array evidence for multi-state non-volatile low-energy operation."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Using a 2D photoconductive material as the photosensitive control gate implements the motivated route toward light-driven control in an FGT architecture."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Multi-level resistive-state capacity is a core training-oriented design target for synaptic devices because training dynamically adjusts many weights and too few states hinder high training accuracy."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) An O-FGT that provides light-adjustable synaptic weights and non-volatile storage after light removal is a plausible device basis for unifying training updates with inference retention."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) A photosensitive 2D control-gate O-FGT architecture supports gate-voltage-free optical weight modulation because the control-gate route lets light play the role of the traditional gate voltage."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) The demonstrated (NH4)BiI3 O-FGT, with gate-voltage-free optical weight tuning and non-volatile storage after light removal, supports the claim that the O-FGT approach can address unified training-update and inference-retention needs."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) The device’s ultra-low update energy and up to 360 resistive states make the O-FGT promising for high-accuracy, low-energy neuromorphic training."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Together, non-volatile gate-voltage-free optical weight tuning and many low-energy resistive states support the O-FGT approach as a candidate for unified training and inference."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) The device’s ultra-low training energy and large number of resistive states support its promise for accurate, energy-efficient neuromorphic training."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) The reported 1T1M ANN result provides task-level evidence that the device-array concept can be evaluated in neuromorphic application settings, without by itself proving broad scalability."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) The array’s GPU-comparable YOLOv8 performance with greatly reduced energy is consistent with the NVOM goal of accurate, fast, and low-energy optoelectronic memory hardware."];
  "R30" [label="(0,0,0) The repaired architecture, material-selection, and device/array-performance evidence jointly support proposing an (NH4)BiI3-based O-FGT NVOM for gate-voltage-free optoelectronic neuromorphic memory."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) Because gate-voltage-free optical control is motivated and the photosensitive-control-gate construction defines the O-FGT route, the O-FGT architecture is a justified component of the proposal."];
  "R33" [label="(0,0,0) Device and array demonstrations of non-volatile optical weight tuning, many low-energy resistive states, ANN operation, and energy-efficient YOLOv8 performance provide feasibility evidence for the proposed NVOM approach."];
  "R39" [label="(0,0,0) (NH4)BiI3 can be included as the selected control-gate material premise because the repaired material reasoning supports its match to the photosensitive-control-gate criteria."];
  "R40" [label="(0,0,0) The repaired demonstration summary can be included as feasibility evidence for the proposal."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because added gate electrodes hinder integration, increase energy consumption, and slow response, a device in which light replaces the traditional gate-voltage role motivates gate-voltage-free optical control."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) The observation that too few resistive states hinder high training accuracy supports increasing the number of adjustable resistive states for training-oriented synaptic devices."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Replacing the metal control gate with a 2D photoconductive photosensitive control gate is the architectural route by which the new device lets light play the role of gate voltage in an O-FGT."];
  "d10" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given 2D materials provide a material basis for FGTs and 2D materials are commonly used as channels or floating gates for carrier storage, therefore existing FGT implementations often place 2D materials in channel/floating-gate roles."];
  "d11" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the control-gate material should have excellent photoconductivity, low EB, and long τ0 and gate-voltage-free exciton separation/write-erase requires low EB and long τ0 with efficient carrier generation/transport, therefore these material criteria are necessary for O-FGT control gates."];
  "d12" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given 2D Pb/Bi halide perovskites are photoconductive and large EB and short τ0 impede exciton separation and transport, therefore these perovskites are inadequate for gate-voltage-free optical control despite photoconductivity."];
  "d14" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the NVOM approach is a plausible foundation and the target hardware should be an optically programmable non-volatile electrical memory, therefore NVOM is an appropriate direction for optical-programmable non-volatile electrical weights."];
  "d16" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given inference requires non-volatile storage and retention after optical pulses is a core constraint, therefore robust non-volatility/retention is a core inference requirement."];
  "d18" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given most 2D materials have poor photoconductivity and the control gate must have high photoconductivity, therefore most 2D materials are unsuitable as O-FGT photosensitive control gates."];
  "d2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the strategy is optical training with electrical non-volatile inference and such a strategy requires optical programming with electrically readable non-volatile weights, therefore target hardware should be an optically programmable non-volatile electrical memory."];
  "d24" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given a feasible O-FGT requires both an architecture choice and a material meeting criteria and (NH4)BiI3 meets the criteria for the photosensitive control gate, therefore an (NH4)BiI3-based O-FGT is a justified concrete instantiation."];
  "d27" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given a dielectric 2D photoconductive material with high DOS, low EB, and long τ0 would meet O-FGT control-gate requirements and (NH4)BiI3 is synthesized with these properties, therefore (NH4)BiI3 realizes the hypothesized material direction."];
  "d29" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given 2D vdW materials provide a basis for FGTs and FGT is ideal for NVOM, therefore 2D-material-enabled FGT architectures are a plausible NVOM implementation path."];
  "d3" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given resistive states should not decay after light removal and inference retention requires non-decaying states, therefore retention after optical pulses is a core constraint."];
  "d30" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the opto-electrical strategy leverages optical bandwidth/speed and electrical integration and an optically programmable non-volatile electrical memory matches this strategy, therefore pursuing optically programmed electrical non-volatile memory aligns with the strategy motivation."];
  "d4" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given FGT is ideal for NVOM and NVOM design can start from an FGT architecture, therefore use an FGT-based design path."];
  "d8" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given low EB and long τ0 enable gate-voltage-free exciton separation and write/erase and light must replace gate voltage, therefore the control gate must have high photoconductivity plus low EB and long τ0."];
  "d9" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the O-FGT control gate needs high photoconductivity, low EB, and long τ0 and (NH4)BiI3 is synthesized with low EB and long τ0 with high DOS supporting photoconductive response, therefore (NH4)BiI3 is a suitable photosensitive control-gate candidate for O-FGT."];
  "iA" [label="(0,0,0) If a hardware synapse can be updated with many fine-grained states during training and retain those states non-volatilely for inference, then it can serve as a unified device for both training and inference."];
  "iB" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given training needs many adjustable resistive states and inference needs non-volatile weights and an NVOM provides light-programmable multi-level non-volatile states, therefore NVOM is a plausible hardware foundation."];
  "iC" [label="(0,0,0) If FGTs are ideal for NVOM, then designing an NVOM can reasonably start from an FGT architecture."];
  "iD" [label="(0,0,0) If an optical-training/electrical-inference strategy is desired, then the memory element should accept optical programming and output/store electrically readable non-volatile weights."];
  "iE" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given conventional 2D FGT roles require gate-voltage assistance for light modulation and a photosensitive control gate would allow light to substitute for gate voltage, therefore using a 2D photoconductive control gate is a plausible solution."];
  "iF" [label="(0,0,0) If light is to replace gate voltage in an O-FGT, then the photosensitive control gate must generate/transport carriers efficiently and allow exciton separation without external gate bias, requiring high photoconductivity, low EB, and long τ0."];
  "iG" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given candidate 2D materials either have poor photoconductivity or have large EB/short τ0 and a dielectric 2D photoconductive material with high DOS, low EB, and long τ0 would meet O-FGT control-gate requirements, therefore synthesizing such a material is a plausible research direction."];
  "ind4" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given the array shows GPU-comparable YOLOv8 performance with much lower energy and such approaches aim to increase speed and reduce energy, therefore the O-FGT NVOM approach is consistent with targeted system-level advantages."];
  "n10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, in most FGT implementations, 2D materials are used as the channel or as the floating gate for carrier storage."];
  "n11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, when 2D materials are used as channel/floating gate, synaptic weights cannot be modulated by light without assistance from a gate voltage."];
  "n12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, adding a gate electrode creates problems: it hinders large-scale integration and increases electrical energy consumption and slows response."];
  "n13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, existing devices suffer from too few resistive states, which hinders building neural networks with high training accuracy."];
  "n14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, the metal control gate is replaced with a 2D photoconductive material as a photosensitive control gate to construct an FGT."];
  "n15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, in the new device, light plays the role that gate voltage plays in a traditional FGT; this device is an optical FGT (O-FGT)."];
  "n16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, the 2D photoconductive control-gate material should have excellent photoconductivity, low exciton binding energy (EB), and long exciton lifetime (τ0)."];
  "n17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, low EB and long τ0 allow excitons to separate via internal potential in the photosensitive control gate (without extra gate voltage), enabling write/erase of charge in the floating gate."];
  "n18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, most 2D materials have poor photoconductivity, so they are unsuitable as the needed photosensitive control gate."];
  "n19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, although 2D Pb/Bi halide perovskites are photoconductive, their large EB and short τ0 impede exciton separation and transport, undermining gate-voltage-free optical control."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, during training, synaptic devices must dynamically adjust weights to generate many resistive states."];
  "n20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, the authors synthesize a dielectric 2D photoconductive material (NH4)BiI3 with high DOS, low EB, and long τ0 simultaneously."];
  "n21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, an O-FGT using (NH4)BiI3 as the photosensitive control gate enables light-adjustable synaptic weights without gate-voltage assistance and provides non-volatile storage with no current decay after light removal, meeting training+inference needs."];
  "n22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, the device shows ultra-low training energy (~5.25 fJ per non-volatile state) and an exceptionally large number of resistive states (up to 360)."];
  "n23" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, a 1T1M array using on-chip trained weights can implement an ANN achieving ~99% accuracy on nonlinear localization."];
  "n24" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, the array achieves GPU-comparable YOLOv8 performance while greatly reducing energy consumption."];
  "n3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, during inference, weights must be stored non-volatilely to efficiently perform matrix multiplication."];
  "n4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, a promising strategy is to use optical signals for training and convert them into non-volatile electrical signals for inference to combine the strengths of both domains."];
  "n5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, this opto-electrical strategy benefits from optical wide bandwidth and high-speed transmission, while electrical outputs ease integration with external circuits."];
  "n6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, non-volatile optoelectronic memories (NVOMs) are expected hardware foundations: they should enable many non-volatile resistive states using ultra-short, ultra-dim light pulses for accuracy, speed, and low energy."];
  "n7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, a key NVOM requirement is retention: each resistive state should not decay after the light pulse is removed."];
  "n8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, floating-gate transistors (FGTs) are considered an ideal device structure for implementing NVOM."];
  "n9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, 2D van der Waals materials (no dangling bonds) reduce surface states and carrier scattering, enabling fast response and providing a materials basis for FGTs."];
  "rRule" [label="(0,0,0) If an O-FGT architecture removes gate-voltage dependence by using a photosensitive 2D control gate and the control-gate material meets photoconductivity/low-EB/long-τ0 criteria and the resulting device provides multi-level non-volatile optical programming suitable for arrays, then the correct research proposal is to develop an (NH4)BiI3-based O-FGT NVOM for optical training and electrical inference."];
  "rcRule2" [label="(0,0,0) If a candidate material satisfies the required control-gate criteria, then it can be included as a selected material premise for the proposal."];
  "rcRule3" [label="(0,0,0) If device- and array-level demonstrations indicate the approach achieves required properties, then feasibility evidence can be included among the premises of the proposal."];
  "n2" -> "iB" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "n6" -> "iB" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "iD" -> "d2" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n4" -> "d2" [label="deduction-case"];
  "iA" -> "d3" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n7" -> "d3" [label="deduction-case"];
  "iC" -> "d4" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n8" -> "d4" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n11" -> "iE" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
